---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { buildWorldPrompt, plainText } from '../../judge/forecast-prompt.ts'
import { cards } from '../data/cards.ts'
import { abundanceMultiple, causeDefinitions, dangerScale, horizon, outcomeDefinitions, potentialScale, scaleDefinitions } from '../data/definitions.ts'
import { countries, regions } from '../data/entities.ts'
import { latestForecastDate, outlook } from '../data/outlook.ts'
import { dangerColors, levelInk, potentialColors, potentialInk } from '../data/palette.ts'
import { outcomeKeys, rootCauses } from '../model/forecast.ts'

const explainers = await getCollection('explainers')
const aiBody = explainers.find((entry) => entry.data.factor === 'ai_progress_index')?.body ?? ''
const prompt = buildWorldPrompt({ cards: cards, curves: outlook.curves, aiNote: plainText({ markdown: aiBody, limit: 2500 }) })
const published = cards.reduce((sum, card) => sum + card.published.noSi.length + card.published.si.length, 0)
const scaled = cards.filter((card) => card.scale !== 'none').length
// Growth needed to multiply a living standard by five over the 75 years to 2100.
const yearsLeft = horizon - 2025
const growthForFive = (abundanceMultiple ** (1 / yearsLeft) - 1) * 100
const doublingAtOne = 1.01 ** yearsLeft
---

<Base title="Methodology" description="How Doom Analysis defines its danger scale, the six outcomes and abundance, builds its cards, and turns them into judged probabilities." active="methodology">
  <article class="prose-site max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="serif text-5xl tracking-tight" style="color: var(--ink)">Methodology</h1>
    <p>
      Doom Analysis has three layers, each labelled. <strong>Cards</strong> measure one thing each (warming, oil, pandemics, fusion), with
      sources, today's value and the danger each value would mean. <strong>Curves</strong> give the chance of each value in {horizon}, taken
      from sources where they exist and judged where they do not. <strong>Forecasts</strong> (doom, the seven causes, the six outcomes of every
      place) are judgments made from the cards under fixed definitions. A measurement never passes for a guess, or the reverse.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scale">One danger scale</h2>
    <p>{scaleDefinitions.danger}</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="data not-prose">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Level</th><th>Name</th><th>Who dies or loses decent conditions</th><th>For scale</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          dangerScale.map((level) => (
            <tr>
              <td><span class="inline-flex h-6 w-6 items-center justify-center rounded text-xs font-semibold" style={`background: ${dangerColors[level.level]}; color: ${levelInk[level.level]}`}>{level.level}</span></td>
              <td>{level.name}</td>
              <td>{level.share}</td>
              <td class="muted">{level.reference}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>{scaleDefinitions.decent} {scaleDefinitions.doom}</p>
    <p>Technologies are read on the mirror image, the potential scale: the share of humanity a technology alone could move up at least one outcome.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="data not-prose">
      <tbody>
        {
          potentialScale.map((level) => (
            <tr>
              <td><span class="inline-flex h-6 w-6 items-center justify-center rounded text-xs font-semibold" style={`background: ${potentialColors[level.level]}; color: ${potentialInk[level.level]}`}>{level.level}</span></td>
              <td>{level.name}</td>
              <td>{level.share}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="outcomes">Six outcomes for every place</h2>
    <p>Each place is judged against its own living standard today, so that getting twice as poor counts as collapse even where nobody starves.</p>
    <ul>
      {outcomeKeys.map((key) => <li>{outcomeDefinitions[key]}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>{scaleDefinitions.livingStandard}</p>

    <h2 id="abundance">Why abundance is set at five times the rich world</h2>
    <p>
      The site exists to test one claim. Elon Musk and others argue that superintelligence and robots will make everything so cheap that work
      becomes optional: "universal high income". Most people in rich countries do not feel they live in abundance today, so abundance has to mean
      far more than today's rich-world comfort.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ordinary growth does not get there. At 1% a year, a living standard grows {doublingAtOne.toFixed(1)}-fold in the {yearsLeft} years to {horizon}:
      that is "better", not abundance. Reaching {abundanceMultiple} times today's rich-world average takes {growthForFive.toFixed(1)}% a year per person,
      every year until {horizon}, starting from the rich world, and far more from anywhere poorer. That is why the bar tests the claim: it is reachable
      with a transformation, not with business as usual.
    </p>

    <h2 id="causes">Seven root causes that add up to 100%</h2>
    <p>{scaleDefinitions.rootCause}</p>
    <ul>
      {rootCauses.map((cause) => <li><strong>{causeDefinitions[cause].name}.</strong> {causeDefinitions[cause].text}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      The judges answer one question per cause and level: what is the chance that the worst thing to happen to the world by {horizon} reaches
      this level, and that this cause started it? Since every possible future has one worst level and one root cause, these chances add up:
      the chance of doom is the sum of the seven causes' chances of doom, and each cause's share is its chance divided by the total.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cards">How a card works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A metric</strong> a reader can picture, with today's value and its source. For events (pandemics, wars, eruptions), the metric is the size of the worst one before {horizon}, such as the share of humanity it kills.</li>
      <li><strong>The danger band</strong> under the axis: what each stretch of values would mean for people, through this card alone. Each stretch is marked <em>published</em> (a source states it), <em>derived</em> (computed from published figures) or <em>our judgment</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>The curve:</strong> the chance of each value in {horizon}, without superintelligence (bars) and with it (line). Every edge of the band has its own stated chance, published or judged; the shape drawn between two edges is only a picture, and no figure on the site is read from it.</li>
      <li><strong>The chance of each level</strong> follows directly from those stated chances.</li>
      <li><strong>Sub-cards</strong> (sea level under warming, oil under energy) explain their parent. The parent's band already includes them, so nothing is counted twice, and a sub-card never shows more danger than its parent.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      There are {cards.length} cards, {scaled} of them on the danger or potential scale, with {published} published points on their curves.
      Each card's page opens with the opening section of a Wikipedia article, quoted word for word from a stated revision under the
      <a class="link" href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> licence, then the site's own numbers and order-of-magnitude
      calculations.
    </p>

    <h2 id="headline">The headline</h2>
    <p>
      P(doom) is the sum of the seven causes' chances of doom, combined over superintelligence by the law of total probability:
      P(doom) = P(SI) × P(doom | SI) + (1 − P(SI)) × P(doom | no SI). P(abundance) is the chance that nine in ten people live in places that
      reach abundance. Each cause page also shows what its own cards give on their own: that figure moves as soon as a card's data changes, the
      judged figure at the next re-judging.
    </p>
    <p>
      The timeline gives, for 2030, 2040, 2050 and 2075, the judges' cumulative chance of each event, rising to the 2100 figures above.
    </p>

    <h2 id="places">Places</h2>
    <p>
      The site covers the world, {regions.length} regions and {countries.length} countries: every country with a population figure and at least
      ten indicators. The regions follow the UN's M49 groupings, with Europe split into North, West, South and East. A region's value for an
      indicator is the population-weighted average of its countries, shown only when countries with data hold at least half of its people.
      Country data comes from a re-runnable pipeline (<code>pdoom/pipeline</code>): the World Bank, IMF, WHO and UN, Our World in Data, and the
      official files of the Global Peace Index 2026, ND-GAIN 2026 and WRI Aqueduct 4.0. When a source has no value, nothing is filled in.
    </p>

    <h2 id="judges">Probabilities: AI judges, run several times</h2>
    <p>
      No dataset can calibrate the chance of collapse in a world with superintelligence, so frontier models act as judges. They receive the fixed
      definitions and the evidence, and answer in strict JSON, in four rounds:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Curves:</strong> the chance of each band edge that no source publishes, for every card, with and without superintelligence. Two independent runs per batch.</li>
      <li><strong>The world and the regions:</strong> superintelligence, the seven causes at levels 3, 4 and 5, world abundance, and the six outcomes of each region. Three independent runs.</li>
      <li><strong>Timeline:</strong> when the world's chances build up between now and 2100. Three independent runs.</li>
      <li><strong>Countries:</strong> the six outcomes, judged against the world and the country's region. Three runs for the most populous countries and Europe's majors, one for the rest.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Runs are averaged, not taken at the median, so that shares that add up to 100% still do. Every run is checked before it reaches the site,
      and an incoherent run is rejected: probabilities outside [0, 1]; a cause whose chance rises with the level; causes that sum to more than 1;
      a place whose outcomes do not sum to 1; a judge's direct estimate of doom far from the sum of its causes; a curve that rises, or that
      contradicts a published point.
    </p>
    <p>
      "Without superintelligence" includes the futures in which a catastrophe is what stopped it. That makes it look worse than a world that
      simply chose not to build it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Current judge: {outlook.model}, last run {latestForecastDate}. Language models are not calibrated forecasters: they lean toward numbers common
      in their training data and are sensitive to wording. Hence the fixed, published prompt, several runs, and every input visible on the page.
    </p>

    <h2>What this does not do yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No backtest.</strong> Scoring the evidence as it stood in 2000 against what happened by 2020 would give the country probabilities a calibrated anchor.</li>
      <li><strong>One judge model.</strong> Other frontier models and human forecasters should answer the same prompts.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-judging is manual.</strong> The headline moves when the judges are re-run; a regular schedule is planned.</li>
      <li><strong>Living standard</strong> is measured by consumption alone; floor area and free time exist for fewer countries.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The world judge prompt</h2>
    <pre class="max-h-[32rem] overflow-y-auto">{prompt}</pre>
  </article>
</Base>
